From 05416a31effbb63e8a24fd0b5946dd724f73592d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Valentin Haudiquet <valentin.haudiquet@canonical.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 23:16:45 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 12/21] video: add MT8183 cold display bring-up for krane with
 BOE panel

Bring up the krane (MT8183) display from a cold start in U-Boot,
instead of reviving the scanout the boot firmware left running:

- mt8183_display.c: OVL pipeline setup, staged bring-up (clocks,
  panel, DSI init, DSI enable, pipeline), framebuffer allocation from
  the top of the DRAM window the control FDT describes, rotated
  console via the panel rotation, dcache flush for the DMA-scanned
  framebuffer, and a firmware-handoff fallback when a stage fails.
  If the pipeline comes up, a probe banner reports the geometry and
  the bring-up time.
- mt8183_disp.c/h: OVL/mutex/MMSYS register helpers and handoff
  scanout discovery.
- panel_boe_tv101wum.c: BOE TV101WUM-NL6 panel driver with the DCS
  init sequence.

The framebuffer is protected with the same scheme as the display
hand-off: fdt_add_mem_rsv() in the control FDT plus
boot_fdt_add_mem_rsv_regions(), which feeds LMB; LMB reservations are
notified into the EFI memory map, so EFI page allocations skip the
region too, and the /memreserve/ entry is honored by the kernel
regardless of which DTB ends up being used (GRUB commonly loads its
own devicetree file, which defeats a /memory reg trim).  The
allocation itself goes through lmb_alloc_mem() from the top of the
/memory window; a failed reservation fails the pipeline stage so
probe falls back to the firmware-handoff path instead of scanning out
of an unprotected framebuffer.

Verified on hardware: cold bring-up, EFI boot to grub and kernel.

Signed-off-by: Valentin Haudiquet <valentin.haudiquet@canonical.com>
